from pathlib import Path
import json,re,html
import pandas as pd
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];P=R.parents[1]
base='/projects/age-prediction/cycles/fewshot/'
c=pd.read_csv(R/'results/learning_curves.csv');s=json.loads((R/'results/summary.json').read_text())
title='Few-shot adaptation of epigenetic age across cohorts'
labels={'source':'Frozen source ridge','median':'Support median','affine':'Affine recalibration','target_ridge':'Target-only ridge','residual_linear':'Linear residual kernel','residual_rbf':'RBF residual kernel','selected_baseline':'Support-CV-selected baseline','meta_no_augmentation':'Neural adapter, no augmentation','meta_augmented':'Neural adapter, augmented'}
table=''
for key,label in labels.items():
 vals=[c[(c.cohort==cohort)&(c.shots==16)&(c.model==key)].iloc[0] for cohort in ['GSE40279','GSE37008']]
 table+='<tr><td>'+label+'</td>'+''.join(f'<td>{v.mae:.3f} ({v.low:.3f}–{v.high:.3f})</td>' for v in vals)+'</tr>'
def fig(name,caption):return f'<figure class="fig"><a href="{base}figures/{name}.svg"><img src="{base}figures/{name}.png" alt="{html.escape(caption)}" loading="lazy"></a><figcaption>{caption} <a href="{base}figures/{name}.pdf">PDF</a> / <a href="{base}figures/{name}.svg">SVG</a></figcaption></figure>'
body=f'''<div class="age-project">
<h3>Abstract</h3>
<p>Epigenetic age predictors are sensitive to population composition, blood-cell source and assay design, yet target-domain labels are often scarce. We evaluated a neural representation trained by episodic optimization and adapted by differentiable ridge regression in two independent methylation cohorts. The source analysis comprised 542 training samples and 92 samples reserved for checkpoint selection; external evaluation comprised 588 query participants separated from support pools of 8, 16 or 32 labelled individuals. At 16 labels, the augmented model achieved mean absolute errors (MAEs) of 7.293 years in whole blood and 3.670 years in peripheral blood mononuclear cells (PBMCs), compared with 7.620 and 4.053 years for support-selected classical comparators. The corresponding paired MAE reductions were 0.326 years (95% conditional interval, 0.185–0.472; Holm-adjusted P = 0.0002) and 0.384 years (0.211–0.573; P = 0.0002). A frozen source ridge model retained lower error in whole blood, and affine recalibration was statistically indistinguishable from the neural adapter in PBMCs. These results identify a reproducible advantage over support-set model selection while delimiting the conditions under which few-shot adaptation improves upon fixed or assay-matched source models.</p>
<h3>Results</h3>
<h4>Source-domain learning and independent external evaluation</h4>
<p>Healthy adult control samples from GSE41037 and control women from GSE19711 provided 634 source samples. Discovery plate H (92 samples) was reserved for source-only checkpoint selection; the remaining 542 samples supplied feature selection, preprocessing, source-clock fitting and neural training. The model used 1,024 CpGs selected exclusively in the source-training partition. The external whole-blood cohort GSE40279 contained 656 participants aged 19–101 years. The PBMC release GSE37008 contained 99 deposited arrays; 92 participants aged 24–45 years remained after the specified quality filters and identifier audit.</p>
<p>Fixed random support pools contained 128 whole-blood participants and 32 PBMC participants, leaving 528 and 60 query participants. Query participants never entered model fitting, support-only baseline selection or checkpoint selection. Twenty seeded support draws were evaluated at each label budget, with the 16-shot comparison specified as primary. The target releases contained observations for 834 and 809 of the 1,024 selected probes; entirely unavailable probes were imputed using source-training medians.</p>
{fig('figure1_cohorts_architecture','Figure 1. Cohorts and neural adaptation. (a) Age distributions of source-training, source-validation and external query participants; empirical cumulative distributions show every observed age without density smoothing. (b) Fixed support-pool and query-set sizes, displayed as within-cohort proportions with participant counts. (c) Source-domain episodic learning through a differentiable ridge solve, followed by support-only adaptation with the encoder frozen. The source clock supplies the initial age estimate and a coordinate in the learned representation.')}
<h4>Few-shot adaptation improves upon support-selected comparators</h4>
<p>At the prespecified 16-label endpoint, the neural adapter reduced MAE by 4.3% in whole blood and 9.5% in PBMCs relative to the comparator selected by cross-validation within each support set. Both paired contrasts remained significant after Holm correction across cohorts. Inference is conditional on the recorded support draws and fixed query populations and therefore does not quantify uncertainty across independently sampled cohorts or subsequent model-development decisions.</p>
<p>The whole-blood advantage was largest at eight labels and decreased with additional target-domain supervision. In PBMCs, neural predictions remained close to affine recalibration across label budgets. The full 16-label comparison is reported below to distinguish the prespecified support-selected contrast from comparisons with individual estimators.</p>
<div class="table-scroll"><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Whole blood MAE, years (95% interval)</th><th>PBMC MAE, years (95% interval)</th></tr></thead><tbody>{table}</tbody></table></div>
{fig('figure2_external_performance','Figure 2. External prediction performance. (a,b) Learning curves at 8, 16 and 32 labelled support participants. Frozen source ridge does not use target labels. (c,d) Prespecified 16-label comparators, including the non-augmented neural model. Conditional 95% intervals were obtained by resampling query participants after averaging absolute losses across support draws.')}
<h4>Performance relative to fixed classical estimators</h4>
<p>In whole blood, frozen source ridge achieved an MAE of 6.842 years, compared with 7.293 years for the augmented neural model; linear and radial-basis-function residual kernels yielded MAEs of 7.038 and 7.052 years, respectively. In PBMCs, affine recalibration achieved an MAE of 3.854 years. The neural improvement relative to affine recalibration was 0.185 years, with a conditional interval that included zero. Thus, the primary result is specific to support-set model selection rather than uniform superiority over each classical estimator. The discrepancy further indicates that cross-validation among candidate adapters is unstable when only 16 target labels are available.</p>
<h4>Augmentation and assay-availability diagnostics</h4>
<p>Removal of synthetic feature augmentation produced MAEs of 7.266 years in whole blood and 3.656 years in PBMCs, marginally below the corresponding augmented estimates. No predictive benefit from the evaluated augmentation distribution was observed. Source-validation trajectories also varied across checkpoints, supporting checkpoint selection on data isolated from episodic training.</p>
<p>A post hoc assay-matching analysis refitted the source ridge model using only probes represented in each target dataset. Regularization was selected by grouped cross-validation in the source data, without access to target ages. The assay-matched model achieved MAEs of 6.094 years in whole blood and 7.893 years in PBMCs; offset-only calibration yielded 7.088 and 4.020 years, respectively. The opposing cohort-specific effects indicate that missing-probe structure and mean calibration shift are insufficient to account for the observed transport error. Because this analysis was specified after the primary evaluation, it is interpreted as a diagnostic rather than confirmatory evidence.</p>
{fig('figure3_robustness_ablation','Figure 3. Statistical contrasts and sensitivity analyses. (a) Prespecified 16-label MAE reductions relative to support-CV-selected comparators, with conditional 95% intervals and Holm-adjusted P values. (b) MAE reductions across 20 overlapping support draws; horizontal segments indicate means. (c) Augmentation ablation; negative values indicate lower error without augmentation. (d) Post hoc assay-matched source-ridge analysis shown with the frozen source clock and 16-label augmented neural model.')}
<h3>Discussion</h3>
<p>Episodic neural adaptation reduced error relative to a prespecified support-selected comparator in two external cohorts, but its performance relative to fixed classical estimators depended on tissue and assay context. In particular, the unadapted source predictor remained strongest in whole blood, whereas neural adaptation was competitive with affine recalibration in PBMCs. These results separate adaptation performance from the reliability of model selection under limited supervision and argue for explicit reporting of both quantities.</p>
<p>Future models should estimate the expected benefit of adaptation conditional on assay coverage, tissue composition and source–target divergence. Any model developed using the present external results would require evaluation in an additional untouched cohort, because GSE40279 and GSE37008 would then constitute development data. The current partitions, predictions and statistical contrasts are therefore retained as a fixed record of this evaluation cycle.</p>
<h3>Methods</h3>
<h4>Preprocessing and source prediction</h4><p>GSE37008 reports normalized M-values, as documented in its <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSM908383">sample-table definition</a>. These were converted to beta values using β = 1/(1 + 2<sup>−M</sup>) before source scaling. An initial PBMC evaluation incorrectly used M-values directly and was invalidated during the final scale audit. The corrected evaluation retained every model weight, hyperparameter grid and support/query partition. The <a href="/projects/age-prediction/cycles/fewshot/results/scale_correction.json">correction record</a> and <a href="/projects/age-prediction/cycles/fewshot/results/input_units_audit.json">input-scale audit</a> document this replacement. Only corrected PBMC results appear here.</p>
<p>Source-training medians imputed missing measurements. Age-correlation screening selected 1,024 eligible CpGs, followed by source-training standardization and clipping at ±8 standard deviations. Source ridge regularization was chosen from α ∈ {{10, 100, 1000}} by group-held-out source validation. Feature selection preceded this internal alpha comparison; its development scores are therefore not unbiased generalization estimates. The external query comparisons remain independent of that selection. Source-training neural episodes used group-out-of-fold clock predictions to construct residual targets. GSE19711 ages refer to recruitment rather than necessarily the exact blood-collection date.</p>
<h4>Differentiable few-shot adaptation</h4>
<p>The encoder comprised linear layers of dimensions 1,024 → 128 → 32 with LayerNorm, GELU and tanh. Its output was concatenated with a learned scaling of the source-clock prediction. For support embeddings H and source-clock residuals r, centered kernel ridge regression fitted a residual correction with a learned positive regularization parameter. The query prediction was the source-clock estimate plus the support-fitted residual intercept and kernel correction. Gradients through the linear solve trained the encoder, regularization and clock-coordinate scale.</p>
<p>Each of six T4 GPU runs used 2,000 source-domain episodes and AdamW, with three seeds per augmentation condition. Episode support and query samples were disjoint. Augmentation applied task-level Gaussian shifts, multiplicative scales and feature masking to encoder inputs while keeping the supplied source-clock values fixed. Twelve fixed 16-shot episodes from the source-validation plate selected checkpoints every 100 training steps. Predictions were ensembled over the three seeds within each condition.</p>
<h4>Classical comparators and statistical analysis</h4>
<p>The baseline family comprised unchanged source ridge, support median, affine recalibration, target-only ridge, linear residual kernel adaptation and RBF residual kernel adaptation. Kernel regularization used α ∈ {{0.01, 0.1, 1, 10}}; four-fold cross-validation within each support set selected hyperparameters and the primary classical comparator. All methods used identical support/query partitions and source-selected input features. The fixed external query labels were accessed only for evaluation.</p>
<p>For each query participant, absolute errors were averaged over 20 support draws. Ten thousand paired participant bootstrap samples supplied conditional intervals; conditional two-sided sign-flip tests supplied P values. Sign-flip inference assumes exchangeability of signed paired losses. Holm correction covered the two primary cohort contrasts, with secondary contrasts corrected as a separate exploratory family. Shared support pools, source-model retraining uncertainty and uncertainty from selecting the fixed support pool are not captured by these intervals. At 32 shots in PBMCs, the complete 32-person pool is reused; only support-CV assignments vary. Two external cohorts do not establish general population-wide validity.</p>
<h3>Code and data availability</h3>
<p><a href="{base}fewshot-reproducibility.zip">Reproducibility archive</a> / <a href="{base}README.md">Protocol and reproduction instructions</a> / <a href="{base}results/design.json">Initial experiment specification</a> / <a href="{base}results/evaluation_lock.json">Evaluation lock</a> / <a href="{base}results/primary_and_secondary_tests.csv">All statistical contrasts</a> / <a href="{base}results/learning_curves.csv">All model metrics</a>. The archive includes trained weights, source-selected numerical matrices, participant partitions, source code, all query predictions, and vector figures. Full GEO matrices remain available from their public accessions.</p>
<pre><code>modal run src/modal_train.py
modal run src/modal_evaluate.py
python src/summarize.py
python src/assay_baseline.py
python src/figures.py</code></pre>
<p>The complete blood–microbiome study appears as <a href="/projects/age-prediction/#iteration-1">Iteration 1 in the main project</a>. Additional training trajectories, probe-availability diagnostics and calibration scatterplots remain available in the <a href="/projects/age-prediction/cycles/fewshot/figures/previous/figure3_robustness_ablation.pdf">extended diagnostic plate</a> and <a href="/projects/age-prediction/cycles/fewshot/figures/previous/figure2_external_performance.pdf">calibration plate</a>.</p>
<h3>References</h3>
<ol><li>Bertinetto et al. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.08136">Meta-learning with differentiable closed-form solvers.</a> ICLR (2019).</li><li><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/42368478/">Bridging the gap: enhancing the generalizability of epigenetic clocks through transfer learning.</a> Relevant epigenetic transfer-learning prior art.</li><li><a href="https://www.scitepress.org/Papers/2023/116244/116244.pdf">Building a DNA methylation aging clock model on less labelled data.</a> BIOINFORMATICS (2023).</li><li>Public GEO datasets: <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE41037">GSE41037</a>, <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE19711">GSE19711</a>, <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE40279">GSE40279</a>, and <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/geo/query/acc.cgi?acc=GSE37008">GSE37008</a>.</li></ol>
</div>'''
body=re.sub(r'<h3>Code and data availability</h3>.*?(?=<h3>|</div>\s*$)', '', body, flags=re.S|re.I)
body=re.sub(r'<a\b[^>]*>\s*(?:PDF|SVG)\s*</a>', '', body, flags=re.I)
body=re.sub(r'<a\b[^>]*>(<img\b[^>]*>)</a>', r'\1', body)
